From 4394e98376fa1451fce7a133ddc63b123f434481 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adrien Delmas <adrien@adriendelmas.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 13:36:45 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 163/180] gsplat: ellipsoid bounds, composite marker that works
 on OptiX

- Splats are bounded by the axis-aligned box of their ellipsoid, cut where
  their bounding sphere was, on Embree and OptiX: a stretched splat's box
  is several times thinner than the sphere's, and every ray that crossed
  the sphere but not the ellipsoid was a wasted intersection call. The
  composite adds nothing below the same response (1e-4), so the image does
  not depend on the shape of the bounds.
- OptiX told the composite ray apart by ray flags, which optixTraverse()
  drops: composited splats rendered black on the GPU. The marker is now a
  pair of payload values no other trace produces.
- Composited splats are kept out of the BVHs that do not composite them
  (CUDA without OptiX, HIP, Metal).

Checked on CPU and OptiX against the numpy reference (4 cases, 0.1 % in
mean, 1 % per block) and in the viewport on a production frame
(4.3 M splats, 128 samples off camera in 25 s on an RTX 3090).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
